# CanvasFlow — Environment Variables Template
# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values.
# NEVER commit .env to git.

# === App ===
NODE_ENV=development

# === Database ===
# Get this from your Neon dashboard. Format:
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DATABASE?sslmode=require
# For pooled connections in production, use the "-pooler" host variant.
DATABASE_URL=

# === Redis ===
# Read by sync-server to fan rooms out across instances. Optional: leave it
# empty to run a single instance, which is the normal local setup. It must be
# set before running more than one replica — including during a rolling
# deploy, where two are briefly live at once.
# Accepts redis:// and rediss:// (TLS).
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# === Auth (added in PR #8) ===
AUTH_SECRET=
JWT_SECRET=

# === Object storage (Cloudflare R2) ===
# Image bytes live here rather than in Postgres: a board is read far more often
# than it is written, and serving pixels from the database bills every view
# against its data-transfer quota. R2 charges nothing for egress.
#
# Leave these blank to run everything except the image routes, which will then
# refuse with a clear message rather than failing at startup.
#
# From the Cloudflare dashboard: R2 > Overview (account id is in the endpoint
# shown there), then R2 > Manage API tokens for a token scoped to one bucket.
#
# The bucket also needs a CORS policy, or the browser's upload is blocked
# before it is sent. Bucket > Settings > CORS Policy:
#   AllowedOrigins: the editor's origin (http://localhost:3002 in development)
#   AllowedMethods: GET, PUT      — GET matters because the download route
#                                   redirects, and the hop to R2 is cross-origin
#   AllowedHeaders: content-type
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
